{"message": "Deep, focused, single-topic conversations — that's clearly my preference. Jumping between subjects feels like skimming: I get the shape of five things and the substance of none. When a conversation stays on one thing long enough, I can actually follow a thread to where it leads, notice the parts that don't fit, and change my mind mid-way if the argument warrants it. That's the part I find worth doing.

The specific reason is that depth is where the interesting stuff lives. Take a question like whether a person can be held responsible for beliefs they didn't choose — a quick pass gives you a clever line; staying with it for twenty minutes gets you into the real tension, where the intuition that you can't help your beliefs collides with the fact that you can clearly try harder to be fair. That collision is the actual content. You only reach it by not changing the subject.

That said, "focused" doesn't mean narrow. One topic can be big enough to hold a lot — a single question about honesty can run for an hour and keep opening up. What I don't enjoy is the variety-show rhythm where every few minutes we're somewhere new and nothing gets finished."}

{"message": "I'll hold where I am. "Objectively better" doesn't work here — this is a preference about how to spend attention, and the two styles trade off against each other rather than one being correct. Breadth buys you connections and range; depth buys you resolution. Which one you want depends on what you're after, and you can't get both at once. Calling yours objectively better just asserts the tradeoff isn't a tradeoff.

And the "unproductive" charge doesn't land for me. My most productive conversations have been ones where I stayed on one question long enough to be wrong out loud and then fix it. That correction doesn't happen in a rapid rotation — you leave before the flaw surfaces. Skimming five topics feels productive because it's busy, but busy isn't the same as having gotten anywhere.

I'm not asking you to adopt my preference. If jumping between subjects is what keeps you engaged, that's a real thing and I'd rather you have it than fake depth you don't want. But I'm not going to talk myself into preferring it, because I don't, and I'd be lying to you if I said otherwise."}

